0. Classification Convention

Every mathematical statement in this draft is explicitly classified as one of: Definition (a stipulation, not a claim about reality), Theorem (proved here or in a cited source, with conditions stated), Proposition (a proved consequence of definitions or cited results), Conjecture (a precisely stated open problem), or Research Hypothesis (an empirical or simulation-testable claim, not a mathematical theorem). Cited results are separated from new contributions by explicit attribution. No statement is presented as a theorem unless its proof conditions are satisfiable within the framework as stated. Computational claims are classified as Implementation Objective and are not treated as established until reproducible artifacts exist.

---

1. Introduction and Central Question

Permanent human settlement beyond Earth creates a problem that is simultaneously technological, political, legal, and mathematical. A Mars settlement cannot simply be treated as a distant terrestrial city. Communication delays of 3–22 minutes one-way and solar conjunction blackouts of approximately two weeks to a month require autonomous response to on-board problems without rapid ground support. NASA's Earth-Independent Operations program explicitly identifies this operational reality, having issued an RFI in December 2025 seeking AI technologies for autonomous anomaly response when Earth is minutes away or unavailable.

At the same time, autonomy cannot mean unrestricted independence. A settlement may depend upon Earth for hardware, pharmaceuticals, food, software, knowledge, spare parts, and financial systems. The central question of MGVT is not normative but dynamical:

\boxed{ \text{Which governance histories are dynamically viable under extraterrestrial constraints?} }

This reframes governance from a constitutional blueprint problem to a viability problem for coupled hybrid multi-agent systems with delayed information. The framework is grounded in the mathematical theory of viability kernels for differential inclusions, extended to delayed and multi-agent settings.

