3. The Hybrid History Space

Definition 3.1 (Hybrid state). The state at time  t  is

z(t) = (x_c(t), x_d(t), \rho(t), A(t), P(t), I(t), q(t))

with components:  x_c \in \mathbb{R}^{n_c}  are continuous physical stocks  (L, E, R, C) ;  x_d \in \mathbb{R}^{n_d}  are continuous institutional variables;  \rho \in \mathbb{R}  is resource concentration;  A \in [0,1]^N  is authority distribution;  P \in \mathbb{R}^+  is power concentration;  I \in [0,1]  is institutional integrity;  q \in Q  is a discrete institutional mode.

Definition 3.2 (Hybrid history space). Let  \tau > 0  be the maximum delay. Define

\mathcal{H} = \mathrm{PC}([-\tau,0], \mathbb{R}^{n_c+n_d}) \times \mathcal{U} \times Q

where  \mathrm{PC}  denotes piecewise-continuous functions and  \mathcal{U}  is the space of admissible control histories. For  t \geq 0 , the history segment  h_t \in \mathcal{H}  is

h_t(\theta) = (z(t+\theta), u(t+\theta), q(t+\theta)), \qquad \theta \in [-\tau,0].

Definition 3.3 (Delayed hybrid dynamics). The dynamics are

\dot{x}(t) \in F(h_t, u(t), d(t)), \qquad q(t^+) = \phi(h_t, u(t), q(t^-)) \text{ at event times}

with  F: \mathcal{H} \times U \times D \to 2^{\mathbb{R}^{n_c+n_d}}  and  d \in D  an exogenous disturbance.

Definition 3.4 (Constraint history set). The constraint set in the history space is

\mathcal{K}_H = \{ h \in \mathcal{H} : z(\theta) \in K \ \forall \theta \in [-\tau,0] \}

where  K = K_S \cap K_R \cap K_P \cap K_I \cap K_E \cap K_C \subseteq Z  is a constraint set on the full hybrid state  z , not on controls or discrete modes.

