5. Controlled Approximation of the Delay Operator

Proposition 5.1 (Padé approximation of the delay — cited). The delay operator  e^{-s\tau}  admits a Padé rational approximation

e^{-s\tau} \approx \frac{P_{m,n}(s\tau)}{Q_{m,n}(s\tau)}.

The Padé approximation of the exponential function is closely related to Kummer hypergeometric functions. Recent work has established a link between the degenerate hypergeometric function and the zeros distribution of the characteristic function of linear delay-differential equations, characterizing the multiplicity-induced-dominancy (MID) property. This provides a spectral interpretation of the approximation. In viability computation, third-order Padé approximation has been used successfully to reduce delayed systems to finite-dimensional discrete-time models amenable to kd-tree kernel algorithms.

Remark 5.2 (What Padé does not establish). Approximation of the delay operator in the frequency domain does not automatically imply Hausdorff approximation of the viability kernel. Viability kernels can have discontinuous dependence on dynamics near bifurcations or loss of invariance.

Conjecture 5.3 (Kernel approximation under Padé reduction). Let  \mathcal{V}^\tau_{\mathrm{MG}}  be the exact delayed viability kernel projected onto the Padé basis, and let  \mathcal{V}^{\text{Padé}}_{\mathrm{MG}}  be the viability kernel of the Padé-reduced system. Under the following assumptions:

1.  F  satisfies the Marchaud property and is Lipschitz continuous with respect to the history variable;
2. The Padé approximation error satisfies  \|e^{-s\tau} - P_{m,n}/Q_{m,n}\|_\infty \leq \epsilon_{m,n}(\tau) ;
3. The viability kernel depends continuously on the dynamics in the Hausdorff metric in a neighborhood of the exact system;

then there exists  C > 0  such that

d_H(\mathcal{V}^\tau_{\mathrm{MG}}, \mathcal{V}^{\text{Padé}}_{\mathrm{MG}}) \leq C \epsilon_{m,n}(\tau).

This is a research objective, not an established result.

