6. Multi-Agent Guaranteed Viability

Definition 6.1 (Individual guaranteed viability kernel — cited). For player  i  in an  N -player dynamic game, the individual guaranteed viability kernel is

\mathcal{V}_i^{\text{guar}} = \{ h \in \mathcal{H} : \exists u_i(\cdot) \text{ s.t. } \forall u_{-i}(\cdot), \ h_t \in K_i \ \forall t \}.

Definition 6.2 (Global viability domain — cited). The global viability domain is

D^* = \bigcup_{x \in L} \left( \{x\} \times \left( \prod_{i=1}^N \left( \mathrm{Guar}_i(\mathcal{L}_i \times L) \cap (\mathbb{R}^{n_i} \times \{x\}) \right) \right) \right)

which gathers all states  (x, (x_i)_{i \in \mathcal{N}})  for which  (x, x_i) \in \mathrm{Guar}_i  for all  i . When the global viability domain is non-empty, it allows the design of individual strategies that ensure the viability of all.

Theorem 6.3 (Collective viability — cited). Under the assumptions of the Martin–Alvarez–Lavallée framework (discrete-time deterministic infinite-horizon  N -player game, non-empty individual guaranteed viability kernels, and appropriate measurability conditions on feedback strategies), the subset  D^*  is a viability domain for the coupled dynamics, and an evolution starting in  D^*  governed by the individual guaranteed strategies is viable for all constraints.

Attribution. This is the central result of Martin, Alvarez, and Lavallée ( Automatica , 2024). The proof is constructive: each non-empty guaranteed kernel yields a feedback strategy ensuring individual viability regardless of others' actions; the simultaneous adoption yields a trajectory in the constraint intersection.

