7. Contraction and Viability: A Corrected Relationship

Definition 7.1 (Contraction). The coupled governance system is contracting in metric  M  if there exists  \lambda > 0  such that for all  x, y ,

\frac{d}{dt} \|x - y\|_M \leq -\lambda \|x - y\|_M.

Proposition 7.2 (Contraction does not imply viability — corrected). Contraction alone does not imply that the guaranteed viability kernels are non-empty. The unique attractor of a contracting system may lie entirely outside the constraint set.

Proof by counterexample. Consider the uncontrolled system  \dot{x} = -x  with constraint  K = \{x : x \geq 1\} . The system is contracting with  \lambda = 1 . The unique trajectory is  x(t) = x_0 e^{-t} \to 0 \notin K . The guaranteed viability kernel is empty for  x_0 < 1 . ∎

Proposition 7.3 (Contraction plus viable attractor — strengthened). If the coupled governance system is contracting in some metric  M  and there exists at least one admissible trajectory  x^*(t)  satisfying:

1.  x^*(t) \in \bigcap_{i=1}^N K_i  for all  t \geq 0 ;
2.  x^*(t)  is an admissible trajectory of the full controlled system;
3. There exists a robustness margin  \lambda > \mathrm{diam}(D)  where  \mathrm{diam}(D)  is the diameter of the disturbance set;
4. The initial state already lies in the relevant constraint set during the transient, or there exists a control that keeps it there,

then the individual guaranteed viability kernels  \mathcal{V}_i^{\text{guar}}  are non-empty for initial histories in a neighborhood of the viable attractor.

Proof. By condition 3, contraction dominates disturbance, so all trajectories converge to  x^*(t)  regardless of  u_{-i}  and  d . For each player  i , define  u_i^*  as the control steering the system toward  x^*(t) . Since all trajectories converge to  x^*(t)  and  x^*(t) \in K_i , there exists finite  T_i  after which  x(t) \in K_i  for all  t \geq T_i . For  t < T_i ,  u_i^*  must keep  x(t) \in K_i  during the transient by condition 4. ∎

