8. Governance Feasible Control Set

Definition 8.1 (Governance Control Barrier Functional). A functional  h_k: \mathcal{H} \to \mathbb{R}  is a Governance Control Barrier Functional for constraint  K_k = \{z : h_k(z) \geq 0\}  if there exists a class- \mathcal{K}_\infty  function  \alpha_k  such that for all  h \in \mathcal{K}_H ,

\sup_{u \in U} \inf_{d \in D} \left[ \frac{\partial h_k}{\partial z} F(h, u, d) \right] \geq -\alpha_k(h_k(h)).

The notion of control barrier functionals extends CBF theory to systems with state delay by introducing functionals on the infinite-dimensional state space, handling multiple and distributed delays in both dynamics and safety conditions. Kiss, Molnar, Ames, and Orosz established this framework for safety-critical control of time-delay systems.

Definition 8.2 (Individual safe control set). For each constraint  k , define

\mathcal{U}_k(h) = \left\{ u \in U : \inf_{d \in D} \left[ \frac{\partial h_k}{\partial z} F(h, u, d) \right] \geq -\alpha_k(h_k(h)) \right\}.

Definition 8.3 (Governance feasible control set). The governance feasible control set is

\boxed{ \mathcal{U}_G(h) = \bigcap_{k \in \{S,R,P,I,E,C\}} \mathcal{U}_k(h). }

Proposition 8.4 (Feasibility of simultaneous governance constraints). If  \mathcal{U}_G(h) \neq \emptyset  for all  h \in \mathcal{K}_H , then the set  K = \bigcap_k K_k  is robustly forward invariant under any controller selecting  u(t) \in \mathcal{U}_G(h_t) .

Proof. For each  k  and each  u \in \mathcal{U}_G(h) \subseteq \mathcal{U}_k(h) , the CBF condition for  k  holds. Since  u \in \mathcal{U}_k(h)  for all  k , all CBF conditions hold simultaneously. By the standard CBF theorem, each  K_k  is forward invariant. The intersection of forward invariant sets is forward invariant. ∎

Definition 8.6 (Governance infeasibility). If  \mathcal{U}_G(h) = \emptyset  for some  h \in \mathcal{K}_H  along every viable trajectory, then no available authority configuration can simultaneously satisfy the governance constraints.

