11. Multi-Settlement Coupling and the Braess-Style Conjecture

Definition 11.1 (Coupled settlement dynamics). The settlements form a coupled system

\dot{x}_i \in F_i(x_i, u_i, x_j, u_j, \tau_{ij}, d_i).

Definition 11.2 (Collective viability kernel). The collective viability kernel is

\boxed{ \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{multi}} = \mathrm{Viab}(K_1 \times \cdots \times K_N, F_{\mathrm{coupled}}). }

Conjecture 11.4 (Braess paradox for viability kernels). There exist coupled settlement dynamics  F_1, F_2  and constraint sets  K_1, K_2  such that the collective viability kernel for the system with inter-settlement link  F_{12}  is strictly smaller than the product  \mathcal{V}_1 \times \mathcal{V}_2  without the link.

Implementation Objective 11.6 (Low-dimensional constructive example). Construct a two-settlement, two-resource toy model in which adding a resource-sharing link empties the collective kernel while the product of individual kernels remains non-empty. Braess-type degradation has been studied in coupled oscillator networks, where adding an edge increases the critical coupling required for stable synchronization. The viability-kernel analogue requires proof.

