12. Hierarchical Viability and the Ethical Constraint

Definition 12.1 (Hierarchical viability). Compute sequentially:

\mathcal{V}_R = \mathrm{Viab}_F(K_R), \qquad \mathcal{V}_{R,S} = \mathrm{Viab}_{F|_{\mathcal{V}_R}}(K_S \cap \mathcal{V}_R), \qquad \ldots

The resulting set  \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{MG}}^{\text{lex}}  is the greatest subset of the constraint intersection from which a viable trajectory exists that never leaves the higher-priority constraints.

Proposition 12.2. If  \mathcal{V}_R = \emptyset , then  \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{MG}} = \emptyset  for any constraint set containing  K_R .

Proof. Immediate: if no viable trajectory exists for  K_R  alone, then no trajectory can exist for any subset containing  K_R . ∎

Remark 12.3. The priority of  K_R  over  K_S  is a normative design choice, not a mathematical necessity.

