13. Formal Verification via Finite Abstraction

Definition 13.1 (Finite abstraction). Partition the continuous state space into finitely many cells  \{\mathcal{C}_i\} . Define a discrete transition system  \mathcal{T} = (S, \to)  with  S = Q \times \{\mathcal{C}_i\} . For every cell  \mathcal{C}_i  and every control  u , if the reachable set  R(\mathcal{C}_i, u)  intersects  \mathcal{C}_j , add transition  \mathcal{C}_i \to \mathcal{C}_j .

Proposition 13.2 (Soundness of finite abstraction). If the partition is such that for every cell  \mathcal{C}_i  and every control  u , the reachable set  R(\mathcal{C}_i, u)  is contained in the union of cells  \mathcal{C}_j  with  \mathcal{C}_i \to \mathcal{C}_j , and if  \phi  is a safety property  (\phi = \Box \psi) , then  \mathcal{T} \models \phi  implies the concrete hybrid system satisfies  \phi .

Proof. By over-approximation, every concrete trajectory corresponds to a path in  \mathcal{T} . If  \mathcal{T} \models \Box \psi , every path satisfies  \psi  at every step. Therefore every concrete trajectory satisfies  \psi . ∎

ATL formulation for rights — corrected.

Rights always preserved:  \langle\!\langle \emptyset \rangle\!\rangle \Box (\text{rights\_preserved}) .

No coalition can force a violation:  \bigwedge_{\Gamma \subseteq \mathcal{N}} \neg \langle\!\langle \Gamma \rangle\!\rangle \Diamond \neg (\text{rights\_preserved}) .

