15. Research Program — Computational Core and Measurement

Phase I: Minimal Viable Computational Core (Priority)

M1 — Single-Settlement Physical Kernel. Implement a single-settlement continuous physical stock model (life-support, energy, critical resources) under fixed delay. Use kd-tree viability algorithms, which represent viability sets with kd-trees and have been shown efficient for dimensions up to 10–100. Publish open-source code, synthetic data, and Hausdorff-distance diagnostics.

M2 — Stripped Delayed Authority Kernel. Implement a Padé-reduced delay model with binary or low-dimensional authority. Padé reduction of delay to finite-dimensional discrete-time models is established practice in viability computation. Compute the viability kernel and compare to M1 to isolate the effect of delay.

Implementation Objective 15.1. Only after M1 and M2 produce reproducible low-dimensional kernels should institutional variables and multi-agent structure be added. Use control barrier function QP safety filters for real-time safety enforcement while the full kernel is computed offline. Explicit CBF-based safety filters have been developed for resource-aware computation.

Phase II: Measurement Operators (Co-Equal Work Package)

Definition 17.1 (Institutional integrity proxy). Define the observable institutional integrity proxy

\hat{I}(t) = w_1 \cdot \mathrm{DL}(t) + w_2 \cdot (1 - \mathrm{CL}(t)) + w_3 \cdot (1 - \mathrm{RA}(t))

where  \mathrm{DL}(t)  is decision-log completeness (fraction of consequential decisions with auditable rationale records),  \mathrm{CL}(t)  is contestation latency (normalized delay between a challenge and a formal response), and  \mathrm{RA}(t)  is role-ambiguity index (fraction of critical functions with multiple or unclear responsible actors). The weights  w_1, w_2, w_3  are calibrated via expert elicitation. Decision logs, regular model audits, and procedural rights of appeal create accountability mechanisms that reinforce institutional guarantees of integrity.

Implementation Objective 17.2. Collaborate with NASA EIO and human research programs to define observable proxies for  \hat{I}(t)  from existing decision-log, telemetry, and communication-latency data. Without these, Research Hypothesis 2.5 cannot be tested.

Phase III: Bipolar Soft-Law Regimes as Exogenous Inputs

Definition 16.1 (Regime-constrained action sets). Let  \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{A}}  denote the Artemis Accords regime and  \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{I}}  the ILRS regime. Define regime-specific feasible action sets  U_{\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{A}}}  and  U_{\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{I}}} , where the former permits resource utilization and safety zones under the Accords' interpretation of OST Article II, and the latter imposes a more restrictive non-appropriation reading.

As of July 2026, the Artemis Accords have 69 signatories, with 10 additions in 2026 alone. The ILRS has expanded more slowly, with 13–17 signatories. Several nations, including Thailand and Senegal, have signed both frameworks.

Implementation Objective 16.2. Compute  \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{MG}}  under both  U_{\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{A}}}  and  U_{\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{I}}} . Report how the volume and critical delay  \tau^\star  change. This treats geopolitics as a testable parameter rather than background noise.

Phase IV: Modularity as Structural Constraint

Definition 18.1 (Modularity constraint). Let  m \geq 1  be the minimum number of independent, geographically or organizationally separated life-support paths. Define the modularity constraint

K_M: \quad m \geq m_{\mathrm{crit}}.

Cockell's work on extraterrestrial liberty argues that physical modularity—multiple independent oxygen, power, and food paths—reduces the risk of tyranny by preventing any single actor from controlling all life-support infrastructure. The initial dependence on a small-scale oxygen plant and greenhouse gives way to multiple redundant infrastructural elements at larger scale.

Proposition 18.2. Increasing  m_{\mathrm{crit}}  expands the individual guaranteed viability kernels  \mathcal{V}_i^{\text{guar}}  and shrinks the region where  K_P  is violated.

Proof sketch. With more independent paths, no single actor can control the full infrastructure, so the VM row-norm maximum decreases while the mean is preserved or increases, reducing the ratio in  K_P . Each player's guaranteed viability increases because they retain access to at least one path regardless of others' actions. ∎

