21. Conclusion

The central contribution of MGVT is the transformation of governance from a normative design problem into a viability problem for hybrid multi-agent systems with delayed authority:

\boxed{ \text{From} \quad \text{"What government should Mars have?"} \quad \text{to} \quad \text{"Which governance histories are dynamically viable?"} }

The central object is the governance viability kernel  \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{MG}} , nested within physical and institutional viability:

\boxed{ \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{MG}} \subseteq \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{plan}} \subseteq \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{phys}} \cap \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{inst}}. }

The central temporal condition is the existence of a feasible authority allocation:

\boxed{ \exists A \in \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{feasible}} : T_D(A) < \min(T_H, T_I). }

The central open problem is the critical governance delay:

\boxed{ \tau^\star = \sup \{ \tau : \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{MG}}(\tau) \neq \emptyset \}. }

