Appendix B: Necula–Popescu–Vrabie Viability Theorem for Delay Evolution Inclusions

B.1 The Abstract Framework

The Necula–Popescu–Vrabie framework establishes a necessary and sufficient condition for a time-dependent closed set to be viable with respect to a delay evolution inclusion governed by a strongly-weakly upper semi-continuous perturbation of a nonlinear  m -dissipative operator. The condition is expressed in terms of tangent sets generalizing tangent vectors in the sense of Bouligand and Severi.

B.2 Formal Statement (Cited)

Theorem B.1 (Necula–Popescu–Vrabie, cited). Let  X  be a Banach space,  A: D(A) \subseteq X \to X  an  m -dissipative operator generating a nonlinear semigroup  S(t) , and  F: \mathbb{R}_+ \times C([-\tau,0]; X) \to X  a strongly-weakly u.s.c. multi-valued perturbation satisfying the Marchaud condition. Let  \mathcal{K} \subseteq C([-\tau,0]; X)  be a non-empty, locally closed set. A necessary and sufficient condition for  \mathcal{K}  to be viable with respect to the delay evolution inclusion

\dot{u}(t) \in A u(t) + F(t, u_t), \quad u_t \in C([-\tau,0]; X)

is that for every  (t, \varphi) \in \mathbb{R}_+ \times \mathcal{K} ,

F(t, \varphi) \cap T_{\mathcal{K}}(\varphi) \neq \emptyset,

where  T_{\mathcal{K}}(\varphi)  denotes the tangent set in the sense of Bouligand and Severi.

Attribution. This result is established in Popescu, Necula, and Vrabie, “Nonlinear Delay Evolution Inclusions on Graphs,” and related papers.

B.3 Adaptation to MGVT

In the MGVT framework, we set:

·  X = \mathbb{R}^{n_c + n_d} \times Q , the hybrid state space;
·  A  is the generator of the linearized physical dynamics;
·  F  is the set-valued map encoding governance dynamics and disturbances;
·  \mathcal{K} = \mathcal{K}_H  is the constraint history set.

The tangent set  T_{\mathcal{K}_H}(h)  in the history space generalizes the contingent cone. The evaluation map  \operatorname{Ev}_0  extracts the velocity component at  \theta = 0 .

Remark B.2. The Necula–Popescu–Vrabie theorem requires the  m -dissipativity of  A  and the Marchaud property of  F . In MGVT, the physical dynamics (life-support, energy, resources) are typically dissipative (resources deplete without replenishment), while the institutional dynamics may not be. This asymmetry requires separate treatment of the physical and institutional components, addressed in §B.4.

B.4 Two-Component Decomposition

We decompose the state into physical and institutional components:

z(t) = (z_{\mathrm{phys}}(t), z_{\mathrm{inst}}(t)) \in \mathbb{R}^{n_c} \times \mathbb{R}^{n_d}.

The dynamics are:

\dot{z}_{\mathrm{phys}}(t) \in F_{\mathrm{phys}}(h_t, u(t), d(t)),

\dot{z}_{\mathrm{inst}}(t) \in F_{\mathrm{inst}}(h_t, u(t)).

Assumption B.3 (Physical dissipativity). The physical dynamics operator  A_{\mathrm{phys}}  is  m -dissipative: for all  z_1, z_2 \in D(A_{\mathrm{phys}}) ,

\langle A_{\mathrm{phys}} z_1 - A_{\mathrm{phys}} z_2, J(z_1 - z_2) \rangle \leq 0

for some duality map  J .

Assumption B.4 (Institutional Marchaud). The institutional perturbation  F_{\mathrm{inst}}  satisfies the Marchaud condition: for all  h \in \mathcal{H} ,

\|F_{\mathrm{inst}}(h)\| := \sup_{v \in F_{\mathrm{inst}}(h)} \|v\| \leq M(1 + \|h\|)

for some constant  M > 0 .

Under Assumptions B.3 and B.4, the Necula–Popescu–Vrabie theorem applies component-wise, and the tangency condition takes the form:

\exists u \in U \ \forall d \in D: \quad (F_{\mathrm{phys}}(h,u,d), F_{\mathrm{inst}}(h,u)) \cap \operatorname{Ev}_0(T_{\mathcal{K}_H}(h)) \neq \emptyset.

