Appendix C: Control Barrier Functionals for Delay Systems — Full Derivation

C.1 The Infinite-Dimensional State Space

For a time-delay system with state delay, the state at time  t  is the history segment

x_t(\theta) = x(t+\theta), \quad \theta \in [-\tau, 0],

which belongs to the Banach space  C([-\tau,0]; \mathbb{R}^n)  equipped with the supremum norm. The control barrier functional framework extends control barrier functions to this infinite-dimensional state space.

C.2 Definition of the Control Barrier Functional

Definition C.1 (Control Barrier Functional, after Kiss–Molnár–Ames–Orosz). A functional  h_k: C([-\tau,0]; \mathbb{R}^n) \to \mathbb{R}  is a control barrier functional for the safe set  \mathcal{C} \subseteq C([-\tau,0]; \mathbb{R}^n)  if there exists a class- \mathcal{K}_\infty  function  \alpha_k  such that for all  x_t \in \mathcal{C} ,

\sup_{u \in U} \inf_{d \in D} \left[ \frac{d}{dt} h_k(x_t) \right] \geq -\alpha_k(h_k(x_t)),

where the time derivative is understood along the system dynamics

\dot{x}(t) = f(x_t, u(t), d(t)).

The framework is able to handle multiple delays and distributed delays both in the dynamics and in the safety condition.

C.3 Affine Constraint on the Control Input

The key result of Kiss et al. is that the CBF condition provides an affine constraint on the control input:

a(x_t) + b(x_t) u \geq 0,

where

a(x_t) = \frac{\partial h_k}{\partial x_t} f(x_t, 0, d) + \alpha_k(h_k(x_t)),

b(x_t) = \frac{\partial h_k}{\partial x_t} g(x_t).

This constraint can be incorporated into optimization problems to synthesize pointwise optimal and provably safe controllers.

C.4 Extension to Governance Constraints

In MGVT, the safe sets  K_k  are defined on the full hybrid state. The control barrier functional for constraint  k  is:

h_k: \mathcal{H} \to \mathbb{R}, \quad h_k(h) = \begin{cases} \geq 0 & \text{if } h \in K_k, \\ < 0 & \text{if } h \notin K_k. \end{cases}

Definition C.2 (Governance Control Barrier Functional). The functional  h_k  is a Governance Control Barrier Functional for constraint  K_k  if

\sup_{u \in U} \inf_{d \in D} \left[ \frac{\partial h_k}{\partial z} F(h, u, d) \right] \geq -\alpha_k(h_k(h)).

C.5 Proof of Proposition 8.4 (Full)

Proposition 8.4. If  \mathcal{U}_G(h) \neq \emptyset  for all  h \in \mathcal{K}_H , then  K = \bigcap_k K_k  is robustly forward invariant under any controller selecting  u(t) \in \mathcal{U}_G(h_t) .

Proof. Let  h \in \mathcal{K}_H  and  u \in \mathcal{U}_G(h) . By Definition 8.3,  \mathcal{U}_G(h) = \bigcap_k \mathcal{U}_k(h) , so  u \in \mathcal{U}_k(h)  for every  k . By Definition 8.2,

\inf_{d \in D} \left[ \frac{\partial h_k}{\partial z} F(h, u, d) \right] \geq -\alpha_k(h_k(h)).

Since this holds for every  k , all CBF conditions hold simultaneously. By the standard CBF theorem, each  K_k  is forward invariant under the dynamics. The intersection of forward invariant sets is forward invariant:

\text{If } z(t) \in K_k \ \forall k \ \forall t, \quad \text{then } z(t) \in \bigcap_k K_k \ \forall t.

Therefore  K = \bigcap_k K_k  is robustly forward invariant. ∎

