Appendix E: Braess Paradox in Coupled Systems — Background and Analogue

E.1 Classical Braess Paradox

In transportation networks, Braess’s paradox states that adding a new road to a network can increase the total travel time for all users. This counter-intuitive result arises because the new link alters the equilibrium flow distribution.

E.2 Braess Paradox in Coupled Oscillator Networks

Braess-type paradoxes have been studied in coupled oscillator networks modeling power grids. Witthaut and Timme revealed that adding new connections in a network does not always facilitate the onset of phase-locked synchronization. A substantial fraction of potential links induce a deleterious dynamical transition, with details systematically depending on the topology of the networks.

More generally, the Braess paradox may occur in any complex coupled system, where the synchronous state may be weakened and sometimes even destroyed by additional couplings. The phenomenon is referred to as an increase in the critical coupling required for the existence of a stable phase-locked synchronized solution.

E.3 Analogue for Viability Kernels

Conjecture 11.4 (Braess paradox for viability kernels). There exist coupled settlement dynamics  F_1, F_2  and constraint sets  K_1, K_2  such that the collective viability kernel for the system with inter-settlement link  F_{12}  is strictly smaller than the product  \mathcal{V}_1 \times \mathcal{V}_2  without the link.

Implementation Objective E.1 (Constructive Example). Construct a two-settlement, two-resource toy model in which adding a resource-sharing link empties the collective kernel while the product of individual kernels remains non-empty.

Proposed Construction. Consider two settlements  i \in \{1,2\} , each with a single resource stock  x_i \in \mathbb{R}_+ . The uncoupled dynamics are:

\dot{x}_i = -c_i x_i + u_i, \quad u_i \in [0, u_{\max}],

with constraint  K_i = \{x_i \geq x_{\min}\} . The uncoupled viability kernel is

\mathcal{V}_i = \{x_i \geq x_{\min}\} \quad \text{if } u_{\max} \geq c_i x_{\min}.

Now add a sharing link: settlement 1 can send flow  \phi \geq 0  to settlement 2, but the flow is subject to a loss factor  \eta \in (0,1) :

\dot{x}_1 = -c_1 x_1 + u_1 - \phi,

\dot{x}_2 = -c_2 x_2 + u_2 + \eta \phi.

The collective viability kernel  \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{multi}}  is the set of  (x_1, x_2)  from which there exists  \phi(\cdot) \geq 0  and  u_i(\cdot)  such that both constraints hold for all  t .

Claim. For suitable choices of  c_1, c_2, \eta, x_{\min}, u_{\max} , the collective kernel  \mathcal{V}_{\mathrm{multi}}  is strictly smaller than  \mathcal{V}_1 \times \mathcal{V}_2 . The mechanism: if settlement 1 is forced to send flow to settlement 2 to keep it viable, it may deplete its own stock below  x_{\min} , violating its own constraint. The link creates a coupling that reduces the set of jointly viable states.

