Appendix F: Guaranteed Viability Kernels — Full Theorem and Proof

F.1 Individual Guaranteed Viability Kernel

Definition F.1 (Guaranteed viability kernel, after Martin–Alvarez–Lavallée). For player  i  in an  N -player dynamic game, the individual guaranteed viability kernel is

\mathcal{V}_i^{\text{guar}} = \{ h \in \mathcal{H} : \exists u_i(\cdot) \text{ s.t. } \forall u_{-i}(\cdot), \ h_t \in K_i \ \forall t \}.

This is the set of states from which one player can ensure its viability over time whatever the other player’s actions.

F.2 Global Viability Domain

Definition F.2 (Global viability domain). The global viability domain is

D^* = \bigcup_{x \in L} \left( \{x\} \times \left( \prod_{i=1}^N \left( \mathrm{Guar}_i(\mathcal{L}_i \times L) \cap (\mathbb{R}^{n_i} \times \{x\}) \right) \right) \right).

This gathers all states  (x, (x_i)_{i \in \mathcal{N}})  for which  (x, x_i) \in \mathrm{Guar}_i  for all  i .

F.3 Collective Viability Theorem

Theorem F.3 (Martin–Alvarez–Lavallée, cited). Under the assumptions of the Martin–Alvarez–Lavallée framework (discrete-time deterministic infinite-horizon  N -player game, non-empty individual guaranteed viability kernels, and appropriate measurability conditions on feedback strategies), the subset  D^*  is a viability domain for the coupled dynamics, and an evolution starting in  D^*  governed by the individual guaranteed strategies is viable for all constraints.

Proof. The proof is constructive. Let  (x(t), (x_i(t))_{i \in \mathcal{N}}) \in D^* . Then for all  i \in \mathcal{N} ,  (x_i(t), x(t)) \in \mathrm{Guar}_i(\mathcal{L}_i \times L) . Choose

(u_i(t))_{i \in \mathcal{N}} \in \prod_{i=1}^N \Gamma_{\mathrm{Guar}_i(\mathcal{L}_i \times L)}(x_i(t), x(t)).

Then  (x_i(t+1), x(t+1)) \in \mathrm{Guar}_i(\mathcal{L}_i \times L)  for all  i \in \mathcal{N} , and therefore  (x(t+1), (x_i(t+1))_{i \in \mathcal{N}}) \in D^* . ∎

Attribution. This is the central result of Martin, Alvarez, and Lavallée, Automatica, 2024.

F.4 Non-Emptiness as a Design Objective

Remark F.4. When at least one individual guaranteed viability kernel is empty, the global viability domain is empty. Ensuring the non-emptiness of the individual guaranteed viability kernels emerges as a way of building collective viability. Designing mechanisms that ensure that all individual guaranteed viability kernels are non-empty is therefore a constitutional design objective.

