Appendix G: Contraction Theory — Proofs and Counterexamples

G.1 Proof of Proposition 7.2

Proposition 7.2. Contraction alone does not imply viability.

Proof by counterexample. Consider the uncontrolled system

\dot{x} = -x

with constraint  K = \{x : x \geq 1\} . The system is contracting with rate  \lambda = 1  since  \partial f/\partial x = -1 < 0 . The unique trajectory is

x(t) = x_0 e^{-t} \to 0 \quad \text{as } t \to \infty.

For  x_0 < 1 , the trajectory never enters  K . For  x_0 \geq 1 , the trajectory leaves  K  when  x(t) < 1 , i.e., at time  t = \ln x_0 . In either case, the guaranteed viability kernel is empty for  x_0 < 1 , and for  x_0 \geq 1  the trajectory is not viable indefinitely. ∎

G.2 Proof of Proposition 7.3 (Strengthened)

Proposition 7.3. Under conditions (1)–(4) of §7, the individual guaranteed viability kernels  \mathcal{V}_i^{\text{guar}}  are non-empty for initial histories in a neighborhood of the viable attractor  x^*(t) .

Proof. By condition (3), there exists a robustness margin  \lambda > \mathrm{diam}(D) , so contraction dominates the disturbance. Therefore, for any initial condition  x_0  and any admissible  u_{-i}  and  d , the trajectory converges to  x^*(t) :

\|x(t) - x^*(t)\| \leq \|x_0 - x^*(0)\| e^{-\lambda t} + \frac{\mathrm{diam}(D)}{\lambda}(1 - e^{-\lambda t}).

Since  \lambda > \mathrm{diam}(D) , the second term is bounded and the trajectory enters a neighborhood of  x^*(t)  in finite time.

For each player  i , define  u_i^*  as the control steering the system toward  x^*(t) . Since  x^*(t) \in K_i  for all  t  (condition 1), there exists finite  T_i  after which  x(t) \in K_i  for all  t \geq T_i . For  t < T_i , condition (4) ensures that  u_i^*  keeps  x(t) \in K_i  during the transient. Therefore, for initial states in a neighborhood of  x^*(t) , the strategy  u_i^*  ensures viability against all  u_{-i}  and  d . Hence  \mathcal{V}_i^{\text{guar}} \neq \emptyset . ∎

