Appendix H: Measurement Operators — Formal Definitions and Calibration

H.1 Institutional Integrity Proxy

Definition H.1 (Institutional integrity proxy). Define

\hat{I}(t) = w_1 \cdot \mathrm{DL}(t) + w_2 \cdot (1 - \mathrm{CL}(t)) + w_3 \cdot (1 - \mathrm{RA}(t))

where:

·  \mathrm{DL}(t) \in [0,1]  is decision-log completeness, defined as the fraction of consequential decisions in the interval  [t-\Delta, t]  for which an auditable rationale record exists;
·  \mathrm{CL}(t) \in [0,1]  is normalized contestation latency, defined as the ratio of the mean time between a formal challenge and a formal response to a reference latency  \tau_{\mathrm{ref}} , clipped to  [0,1] ;
·  \mathrm{RA}(t) \in [0,1]  is the role-ambiguity index, defined as the fraction of critical functions for which multiple or unclear responsible actors exist.

H.2 Weights and Calibration

The weights  w_1, w_2, w_3 \in [0,1]  with  w_1 + w_2 + w_3 = 1  are calibrated via expert elicitation. The calibration procedure is:

1. Identify a panel of  K  experts in space governance, organizational behavior, and mission operations.
2. For each expert  j , elicit pairwise comparisons of the relative importance of  \mathrm{DL} ,  \mathrm{CL} , and  \mathrm{RA} .
3. Compute the eigenvector corresponding to the maximum eigenvalue of the pairwise comparison matrix (AHP method).
4. Average across experts to obtain  (w_1, w_2, w_3) .

H.3 Vulnerability Matrix Estimation

Definition H.2 (Vulnerability matrix). The vulnerability matrix at node  k  has entries

\mathrm{VM}_{ij}^k = \int_0^T \left( \frac{\partial F_{ij}}{\partial u_k} \right)^2 dt

where  F_{ij}  is the flow on edge  (i,j)  and  u_k  is the input at node  k .

Estimation Procedure. The partial derivatives  \partial F_{ij}/\partial u_k  are estimated from telemetry data using finite differences:

\frac{\partial F_{ij}}{\partial u_k} \approx \frac{F_{ij}(u_k + \Delta u_k) - F_{ij}(u_k)}{\Delta u_k}.

In practice, controlled perturbations may not be feasible in an operational settlement. Alternative estimation methods include:

1. System identification: Fit a linearized model  \dot{x} = Ax + Bu  to telemetry data using least squares.
2. Network inference: Use Granger causality or transfer entropy to infer directed influence from node  k  to edge  (i,j) .

